course: everyday
lesson: 506
title: A sibling with a disability or illness, a sibling who left home
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In a Leeds supermarket the strip lights buzz, a trolley crashes, and Toby, who is 7 and autistic, drops to the floor with his hands over his ears, screaming. People stare. A woman tuts: «Somebody wants a good telling-off.» His sister Jasmine, 10, steps in front of him like a goalie and stares right back: «He's autistic, he's okay. It's the lights.» Mum takes him out into the cool air, and ten minutes later he is humming and flapping his hands, happy again. A meltdown is not a tantrum for sweets: it is a brain that has had too much noise and light all at once.
explain: Brothers and sisters of disabled kids notice what others miss. In Fife, Ruaridh, 9, hears the class trip is to a castle with a hundred steps. «Kirsty couldnae go there» (Scots for couldn't) — his big sister uses a wheelchair. Miss says, «Good shout, we'll ask about the lift.» In Cardiff, Dafydd's brother has an epileptic seizure at the park. Dafydd does what Mam taught him: moves the bikes away, doesn't hold him down, checks the time and rings Mam. In Belfast, Roisin learns sign language to chat with her deaf big brother, then teaches her mates «hello», «friend» and «biscuit».
explain: Words can cut. In Seattle a boy uses the r-word, a cruel old word for people with learning disabilities, about Eli's sister Romilly, who has Down syndrome (something you are born with that makes learning slower). Eli, shaking, shouts «Don't you dare!» and goes straight to his teacher. In Melbourne people keep asking Jethro, «What's wrong with your brother?» «Nothing's wrong, he just uses wheels,» says Jethro, and walks on. In Ohio Aidan's mates say his big sister is «faking» when panic attacks keep her home. «It's not fake, no cap, it's anxiety» («no cap» means no lie). In Leeds, Marta tells Dad, «He's not being naughty, he's got ADHD, he just can't sit still sometimes.»
explain: When a sibling is very ill, the whole house tilts towards them. In Birmingham, Precious's brother has leukaemia, a blood cancer, and is in hospital for months. Mum stays with him, Precious lives with Auntie, and everyone asks, «How's your brother?» Nobody asks how SHE is. She feels jealous, then guilty for being jealous. That is normal, and the hospital even has a group just for brothers and sisters.
explain: And sometimes a sibling leaves. In London, Kai-Ahmed's big sister goes off to uni in Leeds; he gets her room, which is «well peng» (London slang: really nice), and still misses her like mad, so he rings her on Sundays. In Bristol, Tilly's brother Liam, 19, walked out after a huge shouting match with Dad, and now nobody says his name at tea. It is not Tilly's fault. She can ask gently, and she can keep loving him.
fact: Autism means a brain that works differently; too much noise or light can cause a meltdown, which is not naughtiness.
fact: Many autistic children flap their hands, rock or hum to calm or enjoy themselves; this is called stimming.
fact: People who stare at a disabled child usually just don't understand; a short calm answer is enough.
fact: Brothers and sisters of disabled children often spot steps, narrow doors and rude looks that others miss.
fact: In a seizure, keep the person safe: move hard things away, never hold them down, put nothing in their mouth, time it and get a grown-up; if it lasts more than five minutes, call 999.
fact: Sign language lets a family talk with a deaf member, and anyone can learn some.
fact: Some words about disabled people are slurs that hurt deeply; telling a teacher is the right move.
fact: Using a wheelchair is not something «wrong» with a person; it is how they get about.
fact: Anxiety and panic attacks are a real illness, not faking; ADHD makes sitting still and focusing hard, and it is not naughtiness either.
fact: When a sibling is very ill, the healthy sibling often gets less attention, and feeling jealous is normal and does not make you bad.
fact: When a sibling leaves home, for uni or after a row, it can be sad and a bit exciting at once; it is never the younger child's fault.
fact: «Couldnae» is Scots for couldn't; «good shout» means good idea; «peng» is London slang for really nice; «no cap» means no lie.
exercise: Why did Toby have a meltdown in the supermarket? | The bright lights and noise were too much for him | He wanted sweets and wasn't allowed any at all | He was showing off in front of the other shoppers | He was tired of walking and wanted to be carried home
exercise: What did Jasmine do when people stared? | Stood in front of him and stared back hard | Pretended she had never seen him before | Told Toby off loudly so they'd stop looking | Ran off to hide behind the cereal shelves
exercise: Which is a calm, short answer a sibling can give to starers? | «He's autistic, he's okay» | «He's ill, so keep away or you'll catch it» | «Sorry, he's just being really naughty today» | «Mind your own business, the lot of you»
exercise: Why did Ruaridh say «Kirsty couldnae go there»? | The castle had loads of steps and she uses a wheelchair | Kirsty hates castles | Kirsty was banned from school trips for being cheeky | The castle was too far for the school bus to reach
exercise: What did the teacher say to Ruaridh's idea? | «Good shout, we'll ask about the lift» | «Then Kirsty will just have to stay at home» | «Stop interrupting, the trip's already booked» | «Castles were never built for wheelchairs»
exercise: During his brother's seizure, which did Dafydd NOT do? | Hold him down tight | Move the bikes away | Check the time | Ring Mam
exercise: Why did Roisin learn sign language? | To chat with her deaf big brother at home | To pass a test at school for a gold star | To have a secret spy code with her mates | Because her brother made her do it as a dare
exercise: What did Eli do when a boy used the r-word about Romilly? | Shouted «Don't you dare» and told his teacher | Laughed along so he wouldn't get picked on too | Called the boy an even worse name straight back | Said nothing and pretended he hadn't heard it
exercise: What is the r-word? | A cruel old word for people with learning disabilities | A friendly nickname for a little sister in the USA | A medical name that doctors still use in hospitals | A word for somebody who runs very fast at sports day
exercise: How did Jethro answer «What's wrong with your brother?» | «Nothing's wrong, he just uses wheels» | «He's broken, he won't ever get better» | «Dunno, ask my mum» | «He hurt himself messing on a trampoline»
exercise: Aidan's sister misses school because of panic attacks. What is true? | Anxiety is a real illness, she isn't faking | She is faking to get out of maths tests | Panic attacks only ever happen to grown-ups | She'd be fine if she simply tried a bit harder
exercise: Why can't Marta's brother sit still at dinner? | He has ADHD, which makes it hard | He's had too many fizzy drinks | He wants Dad to get cross with him on purpose | He's trying to get out of eating his vegetables
exercise: Precious feels jealous of her very ill brother. What is true? | It's normal and doesn't make her bad | It proves she doesn't really love her brother | She should keep the feeling secret forever | Only selfish children ever feel like that
exercise: How does Kai-Ahmed feel after his sister moves to Leeds? | Excited about the room and missing her | Just happy, he never liked her anyway | Furious she left without saying goodbye | Scared she'll come back and want it back
exercise: Tilly's brother left after a row with Dad. Whose fault is it that he left? | Not Tilly's | Tilly's, for not stopping the row between them | Tilly's, because she was too noisy that evening | Probably Tilly's, since nobody will talk about it
exercise: What does «peng» mean in London slang? | Really nice | Really smelly | Really tiny | Really dear (expensive)
test: A girl at the shop: «ur brother's well weird, why's he flapping his hands lol». What could Jasmine say? | «He's autistic, it helps him calm down» | «Yeah he's weird, I'm not even related to him» | «He's doing it to wind you up on purpose» | «He's copying birds 'cos he's been naughty»
test: A lad at the park yells: «he's shaking on the ground, grab his arms and hold him still!» What's right? | Don't hold him; move hard things away and get a grown-up | Hold his arms tight until the shaking stops | Put a spoon in his mouth so he can't bite his tongue | Throw cold water on his face to wake him up
test: The seizure started at 4:58 and stopped at 5:03. Mum's rule: «More than five minutes, ring 999.» Did it go over five minutes? | No, it was exactly five minutes | Yes, about seven minutes | No, it was only about three minutes | Yes, it lasted a bit over five minutes
test: «My cousin reckons deaf people can't talk to anyone». Is that right? | No, many use sign language and anyone can learn some | Yes, deaf people can't talk with anyone at all | Yes, unless you shout really loudly in their ear | No, they can all hear fine if you speak slowly
test: Eli: «that kid used the r-word about my sister, should I just let it go?» | No, tell a teacher, it's a slur and it really hurts | Yes, words can never really hurt anyone | Yes, Romilly didn't hear it, so it's okay | No, find out where he lives and sort him out
test: In a cartoon, the boy in the wheelchair is always sad and waiting to be saved. What's truer? | His wheelchair is just how he gets about | Wheelchair users can never have any fun | Everyone in a wheelchair is badly ill | Wheelchair users always need saving
test: Aidan's mates keep saying «she's faking, no cap» about his sister. What would most likely happen if they kept on? | She'd feel worse and more alone | Her panic attacks would stop for good | She'd thank them for being so honest | Nothing, words don't matter to anyone
test: «why's your dad so mardy with your brother at tea, he's only fidgety». («mardy» means grumpy, in the North.) What could Marta say? | «He's got ADHD, he can't always help it» | «Dad's right, he does it to be naughty» | «Just ignore them and eat faster» | «He should go to his room every night»
test: Precious lives with Auntie for 3 months and visits her brother only on Saturdays. About how many visits is that? | About 12 or 13 | About 3 | About 30 | About 90
test: «Kai's sis moved out, he got her room, lucky him, bet he don't even miss her». Is that the whole truth? | No, you can enjoy the room and miss her too | Yes, getting the room makes missing her stop | Yes, brothers never miss their big sisters | No, he hates the room and wants it gone
test: Tilly, quietly: «Is it my fault Liam left?» What's the truth? | No, it was a grown-up row | Yes, she should have stopped them shouting that night | Yes, kids cause most of the rows in a family | Maybe, if she had been noisy on that day
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a meltdown experienced by an autistic child in a brightly lit shop? | A response to too much noise and light for the brain | A deliberate attempt to obtain confectionery from the parent | A sign that the child requires considerably stricter discipline | A condition liable to be transmitted to nearby shoppers
exam: Identify the most appropriate conduct of a sibling who witnesses a convulsive seizure. | To remove hard objects, refrain from restraint, time it and summon an adult | To restrain the limbs firmly until all movement has entirely ceased | To insert an object between the teeth of the affected person | To withdraw from the scene so as to avoid public embarrassment
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the feelings of a healthy child whose sibling is gravely ill. | Jealousy is common and does not render the child bad | Such a child experiences no feelings of any kind whatsoever | Jealousy demonstrates an absence of love for the sibling | Such feelings must be concealed from every adult
exam: Ascertain the correct attribution of responsibility where an adult sibling departs the family home following a quarrel with a parent. | The younger child bears none | The younger child bears full responsibility for the departure | Responsibility rests with whichever child was present at the time | The younger child ought to have prevented the quarrel occurring
